// The fifth and last place an expected type comes from: a return clause is
// typed by the function's return type -- idc.py passes fn.rettype into
// gen_expr there, exactly as it does for the other four.
//
// It cannot actually be reached today, in either compiler: parse_type takes
// `[]` pairs greedily, so `} return int[] [];` reads the literal's brackets as
// two more type levels and then finds a `;` where the expression should be.
// It is here anyway because the alternative is a rule that is right for four
// of idc.py's five cases, which is the kind of near-miss that becomes a false
// diagnostic the moment the parser stops being greedy.
// The func node keeps the return expression in ni1 and the return type in ns2.
want_ret(int i, int id) {
  string rt = s2_of(i);
  int ok = 0;
  if(k_of(i) == "func" && i1_of(i) == id && ty_is_list(rt) == 1) {
    ok = 1;
  }
} return int ok;

// One step of that search: the name, if this function's node is at or after
// the node being placed, and "" (keep looking) otherwise.

fn_covers(int i, int id) {
  string s = "";
  if((import prog)[i] >= id) {
    s = s1_of((import prog)[i]);
  }
} return string s;

// The line to report. The parser fills nline for statement nodes, which is
// where idc.py reports all but three of these diagnostics, so those land on
// exactly the same line. An expression node has no line of its own and falls
// back to its function's -- the same compromise the structural checks make.
tc_line(int id) {
  int n = (import nline)[id];
  if(n == 0) {
    string node_fn_v = node_fn(id);
    n = func_line(node_fn_v);
  }
} return int n;
